            "text": " in each st around. Join with sl st. (24 dc.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 3 (shell edging)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ch 3. *Work a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "shell"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "shell"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " of 5 tr into the next st, skip 1 st, sl st into the next st, skip 1 st*; repeat from * to * around. Note: if your stitch count does not divide evenly into groups of 3, adjust the starting position to begin on a double-increase st from round 2. Fasten off. (8 shells.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "craftChart",
        "attrs": {
          "slug": "crochet-motif-shell-round"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in tails. The shell edging will curl forward naturally; do not block flat if you want this three-dimensional effect. To flatten the motif, wet-block and pin each shell open against the mat. Both results are useful depending on the project."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Joining"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Shell round motifs join at the sl st points between shells. Work a ch-1 and sl st into the corresponding join point of the adjacent motif before finishing each sl st on the last round."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hand wash in cool water. Reshape by hand and lay flat or pin open as desired. Avoid tumble drying."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The six-point star motif uses a similar principle of sharply concentrated increases at six points, but builds the points over four rounds rather than one edging round. The puff stitch square motif introduces a different raised texture to contrast with the shell."
          }
        ]
      }
